---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import NuclideRef from "../components/NuclideRef.astro";
import { NUCLIDES, halfLifeText } from "../lib/nuclides";
import { BQ_PER_CI } from "../engine";
import { decayGrid, decayGridDays, dosingExamples, fractionAfter, minutesTo, pct1, hMin } from "../lib/guide-tables";
import { prefillPath } from "../lib/restore";
import { u } from "../lib/site";

/** ★★ 가이드 글 — 방사성의약품의 붕괴 보정, Tc-99m·F-18 투여 시각표(2026-10-25 D5-2, 감독 지시 12 ②).
 *  ★ 2026-10-31 Lu-177 표를 더했다 — 「lu 177 decay calculator」 노출 22회 · 순위 60 인데 맞는 쪽이 없었다. 반감기가 날 단위라
 *    15분 간격은 뜻이 없어 6시간 간격 · 14일(반감기 약 2번)까지로 한다.
 *  **숫자를 글자로 적지 않는다** — 표·예제·산문의 비율과 시각은 `lib/guide-tables.ts` 에서 나오고,
 *  같은 함수를 `guide-tables.test.ts` 가 고정한다. 반감기는 자료(IAEA Livechart)의 값이다.
 *  ★ 투여 허용 범위(±10%)는 **예제의 가정**으로만 쓴다 — 규정값이라고 말하지 않는다(기관·국가마다 다르다). */
const TC = decayGrid("Tc-99m", 12);
const F = decayGrid("F-18", 6);
const X = dosingExamples();
const tcT = halfLifeText(NUCLIDES["Tc-99m"]);
const fT = halfLifeText(NUCLIDES["F-18"]);
const fMin = NUCLIDES["F-18"].t_half_s / 60;
const MBQ_PER_MCI = BQ_PER_CI / 1e9 / 1000;
/* 흔한 실수의 크기 — 분과 시간을 섞으면, 그리고 방향을 거꾸로 하면. */
const tc90 = fractionAfter("Tc-99m", 90);
const f90 = fractionAfter("F-18", 90);
const tcPerMin = 1 - fractionAfter("Tc-99m", 1);
const fPerMin = 1 - fractionAfter("F-18", 1);
const f10 = fractionAfter("F-18", 10);
const tcTenth = minutesTo("Tc-99m", 0.1);
const winRatio = NUCLIDES["Tc-99m"].t_half_s / NUCLIDES["F-18"].t_half_s;
const COLS = [":00", ":15", ":30", ":45"];
const LU = decayGridDays("Lu-177", 14);
const luT = halfLifeText(NUCLIDES["Lu-177"]);
const luMT = halfLifeText(NUCLIDES["Lu-177m"]);
const LU_COLS = ["+0 h", "+6 h", "+12 h", "+18 h"];
const luPerDay = 1 - fractionAfter("Lu-177", 1440);
const luPerHour = 1 - fractionAfter("Lu-177", 60);
const luDelay = hMin(minutesTo("Lu-177", 7.4 / 9.0));
const luCalc = prefillPath("decay", { nuclide: "Lu-177", a0: 7.4, unit: "GBq", t: 7, tu: "d" });
---
<Base title="Lu-177, Tc-99m, F-18 decay correction tables"
      description="Remaining-activity tables for Lu-177 (0 to 14 days), Tc-99m and F-18, two dosing examples, common mistakes, and a decay calculator set up for Lu-177."
      schema="TechArticle">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">
    Decay correction for radiopharmaceuticals: Lu-177, Tc-99m and F-18 tables
  </h1>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      A radiopharmaceutical dose is prescribed as an activity at the time of administration, but it is
      measured, labelled and shipped at some other time. Between the two the activity falls, and with
      half-lives of {tcT} for Tc-99m and {fT} for F-18 the change is large enough to matter within a
      morning. This note gives the remaining fraction at 15-minute steps for both nuclides and at 6-hour
      steps for Lu-177, whose half-life of {luT} is counted in days, works two examples of finding when a
      dose can be given, and lists the mistakes that most often put an administered activity outside its
      range. The dose calibrator reading before administration is
      always the measurement that counts; these tables tell you what that reading should be.
    </p>

    <h2>The correction</h2>
    <p>
      Activity decays as A&nbsp;=&nbsp;A₀·2<sup>−t/T½</sup>, where A₀ is the activity at the reference
      time, t the time since then and T½ the half-life. The factor 2<sup>−t/T½</sup> is the remaining
      fraction, and it is all the tables below contain. Two rules cover every use of them:
    </p>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Forward in time, multiply.</strong> The activity at a later time is the reference
        activity multiplied by the fraction for the elapsed time.</li>
      <li><strong>Back in time, divide.</strong> The activity that must be drawn now for a dose needed
        later is the prescribed activity divided by the fraction for the delay.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To find the time at which a given fraction is reached, invert the law:
      t&nbsp;=&nbsp;T½·log₂(A₀/A). The half-lives used here are the evaluated values in the IAEA Livechart
      decay data: {tcT} for Tc-99m, {fMin.toFixed(2)} minutes for F-18 and {luT} for Lu-177. The
      <a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline"> decay calculator</a> uses the same values and gives the
      factor for any interval to more figures than the tables.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tc-99m">Tc-99m remaining fraction, 0 to 12 hours</h2>
    <p>
      Read the row for the whole hours elapsed and the column for the extra minutes. The figure is the
      percentage of the reference activity that remains.
    </p>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Elapsed</th>{COLS.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {TC.map((r) => (
            <tr><td>{r.h} h</td>{COLS.map((_, i) => <td>{r.f[i] === undefined ? "" : pct1(r.f[i])}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      Remaining activity in percent of the reference activity, Tc-99m, T½ = {tcT}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tc-99m loses about {pct1(tcPerMin)}% of its activity per minute, so a quarter of an hour either
      way changes a dose by a few percent, and after six hours half remains. A generator eluate or a kit
      prepared at the start of the day is down to {pct1(TC[12].f[0])}% by the end of a twelve-hour shift,
      and falls to a tenth after {hMin(tcTenth)}.
    </p>

    <h2 id="f-18">F-18 remaining fraction, 0 to 6 hours</h2>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Elapsed</th>{COLS.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {F.map((r) => (
            <tr><td>{r.h} h</td>{COLS.map((_, i) => <td>{r.f[i] === undefined ? "" : pct1(r.f[i])}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      Remaining activity in percent of the reference activity, F-18, T½ = {fMin.toFixed(2)} min.
    </p>
    <p>
      F-18 loses about {pct1(fPerMin)}% per minute. Ten minutes leave {pct1(f10)}%, and an hour
      {" "}{pct1(F[1].f[0])}%. Because the loss is so fast, F-18 doses are usually ordered from the
      supplier with a calibration time and an activity at that time, and the site works out the activity
      at injection from that pair, not from the time the dose arrives.
    </p>

    <h2 id="lu-177">Lu-177 remaining fraction, 0 to 14 days</h2>
    <p>
      A therapy dose of Lu-177 is planned over days, so here the row is the whole days elapsed and the
      column the extra hours.
    </p>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Elapsed</th>{LU_COLS.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {LU.map((r) => (
            <tr><td>{r.d} d</td>{LU_COLS.map((_, i) => <td>{r.f[i] === undefined ? "" : pct1(r.f[i])}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      Remaining activity in percent of the reference activity, Lu-177, T½ = {luT}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lu-177 loses about {pct1(luPerHour)}% per hour and {pct1(luPerDay)}% per day, so an hour's delay
      hardly shows and a day's delay does. A vial holding 9.0&nbsp;GBq at its calibration time reaches a
      prescribed 7.4&nbsp;GBq after t&nbsp;=&nbsp;T½·log₂(9.0/7.4)&nbsp;=&nbsp;{luDelay}, between the
      {" "}1&nbsp;d&nbsp;+18&nbsp;h entry of {pct1(LU[1].f[3])}% and the 2&nbsp;d entry of {pct1(LU[2].f[0])}%.
      For any other interval, the <a href={u(luCalc)} class="underline">decay calculator opens with
      Lu-177 selected</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The table follows Lu-177 alone. Material made directly from Lu-176 also carries the isomer Lu-177m,
      half-life {luMT}, which outlasts it by far and governs the disposal of that waste; the
      {" "}<NuclideRef k="Lu-177" /> sheet describes the two production routes.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example: the latest time a Tc-99m dose can be given</h2>
    <p>
      A syringe is dispensed at 07:45 holding 925&nbsp;MBq of Tc-99m for a study prescribed at
      740&nbsp;MBq. The patient is late. What is the latest time the syringe still holds the prescribed
      activity?
    </p>
    <p>
      The required fraction is 740/925&nbsp;=&nbsp;{(740 / 925).toFixed(3)}, that is
      {" "}{pct1(740 / 925)}%. In the Tc-99m table, 1&nbsp;h&nbsp;45 leaves {pct1(TC[1].f[3])}% and
      2&nbsp;h leaves {pct1(TC[2].f[0])}%, so the answer lies between the two. The exact elapsed time is
      t&nbsp;=&nbsp;T½·log₂(925/740)&nbsp;=&nbsp;{hMin(X.tc.minutes)}, and the latest time is
      {" "}<strong>{X.tc.latest}</strong>. At 09:30 the syringe holds {X.tc.at0930.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq; at
      10:00 it holds {X.tc.at1000.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq, below the prescription.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note the direction of rounding. A latest time is rounded down to the whole minute, because at the
      next minute the activity is already below the target. An earliest time, as in the next example, is
      rounded up.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example: the window for an F-18 dose</h2>
    <p>
      An F-18 dose is calibrated at 370&nbsp;MBq at 09:00. Suppose the department accepts an administered
      activity within ±10% of the prescribed 370&nbsp;MBq, that is between 333 and 407&nbsp;MBq — the
      tolerance itself is set locally and varies. Between which clock times can the dose be given as it
      stands?
    </p>
    <p>
      Before 09:00 the activity is higher than 370&nbsp;MBq. It reaches 407&nbsp;MBq, the upper limit,
      at t&nbsp;=&nbsp;−T½·log₂(407/370), about a quarter of an hour before the calibration time, so the
      earliest whole minute inside the window is <strong>{X.f18.earliest}</strong>. After 09:00 it reaches
      the lower limit of 333&nbsp;MBq at t&nbsp;=&nbsp;T½·log₂(370/333), and the latest whole minute is
      {" "}<strong>{X.f18.latest}</strong>. The window is {X.f18.width} minutes wide. A delay of 45 minutes
      past the calibration time leaves {X.f18.late45.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq, {pct1(X.f18.late45 / 370)}% of
      the prescription — the F-18 table gives the same {pct1(F[0].f[3])}% at 0&nbsp;h&nbsp;:45. To give
      370&nbsp;MBq at 09:45 the dose would have had to be calibrated at {X.f18.drawFor0945.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq
      for 09:00.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same tolerance around a Tc-99m dose gives a window {winRatio.toFixed(1)} times wider — the ratio of
      the two half-lives — which is why a late-running schedule is a much greater concern for PET than for
      conventional nuclear medicine.
    </p>

    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <h3>Using the wrong reference time</h3>
    <p>
      A dose label carries an activity and the time it applies to. That reference time is not
      necessarily the time of dispensing, of delivery or of measurement, and suppliers state the
      calibration time separately for exactly this reason. Correcting from the delivery time instead of
      the calibration time shifts every later figure by the delay between the two. Write the clock time
      next to every measured activity, on the label and in the record, and correct from that pair.
    </p>
    <h3>Mixing minutes and hours</h3>
    <p>
      The elapsed time and the half-life must be in the same unit. Entering 90 for an elapsed time of
      90 minutes with the Tc-99m half-life in hours treats it as 90 hours. The correct fraction after
      90 minutes is {pct1(tc90)}% for Tc-99m and {pct1(f90)}% for F-18. A result that is almost zero,
      or a fraction above one, is the sign of this mistake.
    </p>
    <h3>Correcting in the wrong direction</h3>
    <p>
      Multiplying when you should divide gives a dose that is low by the square of the factor. For an
      F-18 dose drawn an hour ahead, the right activity is the prescription divided by
      {" "}{(F[1].f[0]).toFixed(3)}; multiplying instead leaves the patient with {pct1(F[1].f[0] ** 2)}% of
      the prescribed activity. A quick check is that the activity drawn ahead must always be larger than
      the prescription.
    </p>
    <h3>Mixing activity units</h3>
    <p>
      Many prescriptions and older records are in millicuries, and dose calibrators can display either
      unit. One millicurie is exactly {MBQ_PER_MCI.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq; a 20&nbsp;mCi dose is
      {" "}{(20 * MBQ_PER_MCI).toFixed(0)}&nbsp;MBq. Confirm the unit on the display before reading it.
      The <a href={u("/units/")} class="underline">unit converter</a> gives the exact factor.
    </p>
    <h3>Clock and time-zone errors</h3>
    <p>
      A dose shipped from a supplier may be labelled with a clock that differs from the department's,
      and the change to and from summer time moves the clock by an hour without moving the decay. Use
      one clock for the whole calculation, and use the 24-hour clock so that 01:00 and 13:00 are not
      confused.
    </p>
    <h3>Forgetting what stays in the syringe</h3>
    <p>
      The activity administered is the activity in the syringe before injection minus what remains in
      it afterwards. The residual is measured in the dose calibrator after injection where the procedure
      requires it; decay correction cannot account for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline">Decay</a> — the activity after any interval, the time
        to reach a target, and a half-life from two measurements.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/units/")} class="underline">Unit converter</a> — becquerels and curies with the
        exact factor.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/positron-emitters/")} class="underline">Positron emitters and 511 keV</a> — shielding
        F-18 and its decay through a working day.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/half-value-layers/")} class="underline">Half-value and tenth-value layers</a> —
        how much lead a Tc-99m syringe needs.</li>
      <li><NuclideRef k="Lu-177" />, <NuclideRef k="Tc-99m" /> and <NuclideRef k="F-18" /> — the full data pages.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
